Four ideas in this fork came from elsewhere and are named here as well as in the
comments where they are used:

  tylersue/claude-grok-delegation (MIT) — the shape of the read-only barrier:
  confining the agent at the tool layer, with deny rules alongside, rather than
  relying on the sandbox, and the practice of stating out loud when a requested
  confinement layer is not actually enforced. No code was copied. That project
  pairs a positive allowlist with its deny rules; this fork instead removes the
  shell and file-edit tools outright, together with the two MCP meta-tools that
  could reach a writing server. It also does not fail open when the sandbox is
  unavailable, and it keeps web_search available in read-only runs. The reasoning,
  not the implementation, is what was borrowed.

  tylersue/claude-grok-delegation (MIT), a second time — for pointing out that the
  grok CLI has a telemetry and trace-upload channel of its own, separate from
  inference and untouched by any bridge. SECURITY.md documents it because of that
  project. The switches listed there were verified against the installed CLI rather
  than copied, and the wording is this fork's own.

  xai-org/grok-build-plugin-cc issue #4, "Untracked-file review context follows
  symlinks, leaking out-of-repo file contents to the model" — reported upstream by
  GitHub user oneshot2001 on 2026-07-16. This fork carried the same defect and
  fixed it after reading that report. The fix here is its own; the problem was not.

  xai-org/grok-build-plugin-cc issue #3, "`stop` kills process trees before
  confirming its cancellation claim (completed / PID-reuse race)" — reported by the
  same author on the same day. The condition it names was wrong here too, and the
  fix in this fork is its own.
